{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"powdery-wheat-mule","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Why\n\nrebar's `_store/lock.py` mkdir-lock reclamation (`_mkdir_lock_is_stale`) is deliberately\nconservative: every branch that cannot POSITIVELY prove the owner is a dead same-host process\nreturns `False` (never reclaim) — the \"refuse without proof\" contract of bug yaw-gravel-linen.\n304e (castoff-tigerseye-ammonite) fixed ONE instance of the resulting failure — a container\nrecreate making a same-host orphan look foreign — but left the CLASS open: an absent/unreadable\nowner stamp, a malformed v2 stamp, a genuinely foreign-host stamp on a shared filesystem, or a\ncross-namespace stamp seen without the fcntl proof all still wedge the store PERMANENTLY, exactly\nas the 2026-07-31 production incident did (review-bot boot 62s vs a 30s health check).\n\nThe 9305 OSS research (rec #1, highest value, fully backed) prescribes the missing piece: a\nwall-clock AGE CEILING, following git gc's gc.pid 12h precedent (builtin/gc.c) — a stamp we cannot\nprove orphaned is respected UNTIL a generous ceiling, then reclaimed, so the store can never wedge\nforever. rebar holds the write lock only for a single event append (seconds), so the ceiling can be\nfar tighter than git's 12h.\n\n## What / Approach\n\nAdd `_MKDIR_LOCK_STALE_CEILING_S` (1 hour) and reclaim a contended mkdir lock, even without proof of\ndeath, once its lock-dir mtime (the acquisition time; the stamp is never refreshed) exceeds the\nceiling — but ONLY on the branches that today refuse for LACK of a liveness signal:\nabsent/unreadable stamp, malformed v2 stamp, foreign host identity, cross-namespace without the\nfcntl proof, and non-numeric pid.\n\nThe ceiling must NOT override a positive `pid alive` probe. Per the research's own rule — \"if you\never break a lock, do it under a conservative fail-closed guard PLUS a wall-clock backstop, NEVER ON\nA TIMER ALONE\" — a branch where `os.kill(pid,0)` says the owner is alive keeps returning `False`\nregardless of age. The macOS \"pid alive but start-time unconfirmable\" gap is a SEPARATE research\nrecommendation (#6, start-time fallback) and is explicitly out of scope here.\n\nReclaiming an unprovable-but-aged lock is noteworthy, so `_reclaim_mkdir_lock` logs the holder stamp\nand the dir age at WARNING (research rec #8, \"report the holder\"), turning a silent 60s wedge into an\nobservable, attributable reclaim.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_store/lock.py` — the ceiling constant, an age helper, the age backstop on the\n  refuse-without-proof branches, and holder-disclosure logging on reclaim.\n- `tests/unit/test_lock_age_ceiling.py` — new.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An aged-out mkdir lock with an ABSENT/unreadable owner stamp is reclaimed (was permanent):\n      `_mkdir_lock_is_stale` returns True once the dir mtime exceeds the ceiling, and `acquire`\n      succeeds promptly.\n- [ ] An aged-out FOREIGN-host v2 stamp and an aged-out MALFORMED v2 stamp are likewise reclaimed,\n      while the SAME stamps with a fresh mtime are still refused — proving only age, not identity,\n      changed the decision.\n- [ ] SAFETY: a lock whose stamped pid is a LIVE same-host, same-namespace, start-time-matching\n      owner is NEVER reclaimed even when its mtime exceeds the ceiling (proof of life beats age).\n- [ ] SAFETY: a lock whose pid probes ALIVE but whose start time is unconfirmable is NOT reclaimed\n      by the ceiling (the \"never on a timer alone\" boundary; macOS start-time is rec #6, not here).\n- [ ] `_reclaim_mkdir_lock` emits a WARNING naming the reclaimed owner stamp and the dir age.\n- [ ] The existing lock suites (`test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`, `test_lock_container_recreate.py`)\n      still pass unchanged — fresh (non-aged) orphans keep their current behaviour.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and the default suite exit zero.","id":"5389-1633-358e-44f9","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Age ceiling on refuse-without-proof mkdir-lock branches: close the permanent-wedge class (9305 rec #1)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785547416726082001,"uuid":"e4884079-7fb0-4f00-b42d-c1a6c40f8ca6"}